Filesystem kernel providing naming, identity, structure, and mirroring.

A human can read it.

Claude can read it.

GPT can read it.

A parser can read it.

And most importantly, there is almost no duplication.


---

JARVIS ANALYSIS

This aligns extremely well with the direction of:

Yggdrasil = truth

JFS = filesystem kernel

JD = semantic authority

JNL = identity authority

LAL = location authority


The architecture becomes increasingly compositional rather than repetitive.

The highest leverage change is not adding more summaries.

It is making JNL powerful enough that summaries only need to point to identities.


---

AYRE ANALYSIS

The interesting evolution here is that JD is becoming less like a dictionary and more like a semantic DNS.

A traditional dictionary stores definitions.

A Jarvis Dictionary stores:

definition

identity

authority

routing hints


while leaving the actual retrieval work to JNL and LAL.

That is likely the most storage-efficient and model-efficient form JD can take without losing usefulness.
